        <p class="lead"><strong>The Pomodoro Technique is a focus method: you work in a 25-minute block called a pomodoro, take a 5-minute break, and after four pomodoros take a longer break of 15 to 30 minutes.</strong> Its real power is not the timer but a single rule: a pomodoro is indivisible, so if an interruption breaks it, the pomodoro is void and you start fresh. The Best Answer Hub <a href="/tools/pomodoro-timer/">Pomodoro Timer</a> runs the method in your browser, tags each block to a task, and lets you export a dated report, with no signup and nothing stored on a server.</p>

        <p>This guide covers where the technique came from, the exact method, the rule most people skip, what the research honestly supports, and how to pick your interval. The tool sits in the Best Answer Hub <a href="/tools/">Tools hub</a>, is built and maintained by <a href="/about/shahbaz-ali-malik/">Shahbaz Ali Malik</a>, and stays free because Best Answer Hub is funded by optional paid assessments rather than advertising.</p>

