          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="cav">Where it came from</div>
        <h2 id="technique">What is the Pomodoro Technique?</h2>
        <p>The Pomodoro Technique is a time-management method developed by Francesco Cirillo in the late 1980s, when he was a university student using a tomato-shaped kitchen timer to structure his study sessions. "Pomodoro" is the Italian word for tomato, and the name stuck to the method (<a href="https://www.pomodorotechnique.com/francesco-cirillo/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Francesco Cirillo</a>). The structure is simple and fixed: in Cirillo's own words, "the traditional Pomodoro is 30 minutes long: 25 minutes of work plus a 5-minute break," and "every four Pomodoros, stop the activity you are working on and take a longer break, from 15 to 30 minutes" (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pomodoro_Technique" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pomodoro Technique, per Cirillo</a>).</p>
