        <div class="cav">The part people miss</div>
        <h2 id="rule">The one rule most people skip</h2>
        <p>The rule that makes the method work is that a pomodoro cannot be split or interrupted. Cirillo is blunt about it: "A Pomodoro can't be interrupted; it marks 25 minutes of pure work. A Pomodoro can't be split up; there is no such thing as half of a Pomodoro." And if an interruption wins, the block does not just pause, it is discarded: "If a Pomodoro is definitively interrupted by someone or something, that Pomodoro should be considered void, as if it had never been set; then you should make a fresh start with a new Pomodoro" (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pomodoro_Technique" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pomodoro Technique, per Cirillo</a>).</p>
        <blockquote>A Pomodoro can't be interrupted; it marks 25 minutes of pure work. <span class="accent">If interrupted, it is void, as if it had never been set.</span><cite>Francesco Cirillo, The Pomodoro Technique</cite></blockquote>
        <p>That sounds strict, and it is the point. The method's stated purpose is "to reduce the effect of internal and external interruptions of attention and flow." When a distraction shows up, you write it down and deal with it in the next break rather than breaking the block, which flips the habit: interruptions start depending on you, instead of you depending on them. Naming each pomodoro to a task, which the Best Answer Hub Pomodoro Timer does, makes that discipline easier to keep.</p>

        <div class="cav">The honest answer</div>
        <h2 id="science">Does the Pomodoro Technique actually work?</h2>
        <p>There is no large trial proving the 25/5 split is optimal, but the two principles behind it are well supported. First, short scheduled breaks help sustain attention. A study in the journal Cognition found that brief mental breaks during a long task prevented the "vigilance decrement," the steady drop in performance over time, because briefly stepping away and returning reactivates your focus on the goal (<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21211793/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ariga and Lleras, Cognition, 2011</a>).</p>
        <p>Second, protecting a block from interruptions matters for more than time. Research presented at the CHI conference found that interrupted work is often completed faster, because people compensate, but "at a price": participants reported significantly higher stress, frustration, effort, and pressure (<a href="https://ics.uci.edu/~gmark/chi08-mark.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mark, Gudith and Klocke, CHI 2008</a>). A pomodoro protects the block, so the work costs you less stress. Neither study is a test of the Pomodoro Technique itself, so treat them as support for the ideas of breaks and protected focus, not as proof of a magic number.</p>
        <div class="callout peach"><div class="h">A stat worth not repeating</div><p>You will often read that it takes "23 minutes and 15 seconds" to refocus after an interruption. That figure is widely attributed to interruption research but does not appear in the published study, which actually found interrupted tasks were finished slightly faster. It is a good example of a viral number worth skipping.</p></div>

        <div class="cav">Pick your interval</div>
        <h2 id="length">How long should a pomodoro be?</h2>
        <p>Twenty-five minutes is the traditional starting point, not a law. Cirillo frames the technique as a system for awareness rather than a fixed timer setting, and many people adjust the block to the work: shorter for shallow admin tasks, longer for deep work. Common variations include a 50/10 split for longer focus sessions, and some people prefer to follow their own energy rather than a set number.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Preset</th><th>Focus / break</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">Quick Tasks</td><td><span class="score lv1">15 / 3</span></td><td>Admin, email, small chores</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Standard</td><td><span class="score lv2">25 / 5</span></td><td>The classic pomodoro, most work</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Deep Work</td><td><span class="score lv3">50 / 10</span></td><td>Writing, coding, hard problems</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>The Best Answer Hub Pomodoro Timer ships with these three presets and a custom option, so you can match the block to the task instead of forcing every task into 25 minutes. The habit matters more than the exact number.</p>

        <div class="cav">Why this one</div>
        <h2 id="different">How is Best Answer Hub different from other Pomodoro apps?</h2>
        <p>The difference is that it asks for nothing. Several popular focus tools want an account, an install, or a subscription: Forest is a paid mobile app built around a synced account and gamified trees, and Toggl Track puts its pomodoro inside a sign-up-required time tracker with a browser extension. The Best Answer Hub Pomodoro Timer opens in a browser tab, needs no login, stores nothing on a server, and still gives you task tracking and an exportable report.</p>
        <table>
